(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-3-antiarrhythmic-classes-1a-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Antiarrhythmic Classes & Class IA Drugs Quiz 2 — Pharmacology I Exam 3","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic class is listed for life-threatening ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation and for refractory supraventricular tachycardia?","choices":["Class IB","Class IV","Class IC","Class II"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Class IC (flecainide, propafenone) is reserved for life-threatening ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation and refractory supraventricular tachycardia. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Procainamide's acetylated metabolite, N-acetylprocainamide (NAPA), adds activity of which class?","choices":["Class I","Class II","Class III","Class IV"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The active acetylated metabolite has class III activity, so procainamide carries a blend of class IA and class III action. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which class IA drug has the strongest anticholinergic action?","choices":["Disopyramide","Quinidine","Procainamide","Lidocaine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Disopyramide's effects are similar to quinidine but with more anticholinergic action, plus a negative inotropic effect. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of quinidine follows from its alpha-blocking action at higher doses?","choices":["Thrombocytopenia","Cinchonism","Diarrhea","Hypotension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Blocking alpha receptors at higher doses relaxes blood vessels, and hypotension is on quinidine's toxicity list. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A patient taking quinidine reports tinnitus, dizziness and blurred vision. Which named effect is this?","choices":["Lupus-like syndrome","Cinchonism","Torsades de pointes","Thrombocytopenia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cinchonism is tinnitus, dizziness and blurred vision, on quinidine's toxicity list; quinidine is the D-isomer of quinine. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Besides a lupus-like syndrome, which serious effect is listed for procainamide?","choices":["Bone marrow suppression","Cinchonism","Pulmonary fibrosis","Nystagmus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Procainamide's adverse effects include bone marrow suppression (0.2 percent), along with hypotension, slowed conduction, nausea and torsades de pointes. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which blood disorder on quinidine's toxicity list may be fatal?","choices":["Agranulocytosis","Thrombocytopenia","Bone marrow suppression","Megaloblastic anemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Quinidine's toxicity list includes thrombocytopenia, marked as potentially fatal. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which antiarrhythmic class is listed for supraventricular tachycardia alone?","choices":["Class IV","Class IB","Class III","Class IA"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Class IV (verapamil, diltiazem) is listed for supraventricular tachycardia, and verapamil and diltiazem are to be avoided in ventricular arrhythmias. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Which cardiac condition is a listed contraindication to disopyramide?","choices":["Ventricular tachycardia","Atrial flutter","Atrial fibrillation","Heart block"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Heart block, hypersensitivity and glaucoma are the listed contraindications; disopyramide itself causes conduction disturbances. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Quinidine is an isomer of which compound?","choices":["Procaine","Quinine","Lidocaine","Digitalis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Quinidine is the D-isomer of quinine, which links it to cinchonism (tinnitus, dizziness, blurred vision). — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Increasing the energy required for fibrillation is attributed to which antiarrhythmic mechanism?","choices":["Sodium channel blockade","Potassium channel blockade","Calcium channel blockade","Beta receptor blockade"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Beta receptor blockade prolongs atrioventricular refractoriness, prolongs conduction and increases the energy required for fibrillation. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which three drugs make up class IA?","choices":["Quinidine, lidocaine, flecainide","Procainamide, propafenone, sotalol","Disopyramide, diltiazem, dofetilide","Quinidine, procainamide, disopyramide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The class IA drugs are quinidine, procainamide and disopyramide (remembered as PDQ). — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A patient has a new arrhythmia along with hypoxia and an electrolyte disturbance. Which step comes first in the principles of antiarrhythmic therapy?","choices":["Correct the precipitating factors","Start a class III drug to prevent recurrence","Start a beta blocker to control the rate","Start a class IA drug to restore rhythm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The first principle is to identify and remove precipitating factors such as hypoxia, ischemia and electrolyte disturbances before the goal of drug therapy is set. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"Which direct action belongs to quinidine?","choices":["It shortens phase 3","It increases automaticity","It prolongs the refractory period","It lowers the excitability threshold"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Quinidine slows conduction, prolongs the refractory period, slows the rise of phase 0, increases the excitability threshold and decreases automaticity. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"In which state of the cardiac sodium channel does sodium flow into the cell?","choices":["Resting","Inactivated","Activated","Recovering"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When an action potential arrives the channel is activated: it opens, sodium flows in, and the membrane potential shoots up in phase 0. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"Which adverse effect of procainamide occurs in 25 to 50 percent of patients?","choices":["Bone marrow suppression","Lupus-like syndrome","Cinchonism","Torsades de pointes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A lupus-like syndrome develops in 25 to 50 percent of patients, more often in slow acetylators. — Antiarrhythmics.pptx, Slide 13"}]};
